{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787234383,"nonce":"UW3obAFhShiFX+MafyiKjmkmltM=","title":"**What is wrong.** A Slack press by an **unlinked** member takes no idempotency claim, so a Slack","message":"redelivery of that press executes the snooze twice and appends the timeline events twice.\n`slackIdempotency` (`internal/app/adapters.go:1352`) returns the unkeyed zero `Idempotency{}` when\n`uuid.Parse(actorID)` fails — which it does for a raw Slack handle like `U024BE7LH` — because\n`idempotency_claims.principal_id` is `NOT NULL` and `Claim.validate` refuses `uuid.Nil`.\n`Service.Snooze` then skips its claim entirely: `internal/alerts/service/actions.go:419` guards the\nwhole `idempotency.Resolve` block on `if idem.Keyed`.\n\n**The demonstration.** An unlinked member presses `Snooze 1h`; Slack's ack times out and the\ninteraction is redelivered. Both executions run. Each closes the incumbent as `superseded` and\ninserts a new `alert_snoozes` row, so the Case timeline carries two `alert.unsnoozed(superseded)` and\ntwo `alert.snoozed` events for one human press. `TestARedeliveredPressByAnUnlinkedMemberAnnouncesOnce`\n(`internal/alerts/service/snooze_announce_test.go`) already asserts this double execution as current\nbehaviour — it pins that the two snooze rows differ — and asserts only that the CARD is single.\n\n**Why it matters.** Unlinked is a first-class state, not a broken one:\n`internal/identity/domain/slack_identity.go:61-64` — *\"An UNLINKED identity (UserID zero) is a\nfirst-class state, not a broken one: it still acks… Requiring a link before an ack could be recorded\nwould mean the product silently loses acknowledgements from anybody who has not onboarded.\"* So this\nis a normal path, and the timeline is the audit record oto sells. Commit `8b56e45` removed the\naccident that was hiding it: the old constant-`state_version` §C.7 key made the duplicate card\ncollapse, and with an occasion in the key the card is deduped deliberately while the events are not.\n\n**Where.** `internal/app/adapters.go:1352-1364` · `internal/alerts/service/actions.go:419` ·\n`internal/platform/idempotency/claim.go` (`Claim.validate`) · `db/migrations/00041` (which anticipates\na `slack` principal *\"deliberately NOT a FK to users\"* but never says where its uuid comes from) ·\n`db/migrations/00003_identity.sql:48-62` (`users.email` is `NOT NULL` under `users_email_ck`, and\n`users_email_uniq` is `UNIQUE (org_id, email)`).\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** An unlinked Slack presser has a claim principal, so a redelivered press writes one\nsnooze row and one pair of events. **Owner ruling, 2026-08-20:** a shadow oto user is created on\nfirst press, and it carries **no email** — `users.email` becomes nullable rather than holding a\nsynthetic address. That means a migration relaxing `NOT NULL` and widening `users_email_ck` to\n`email IS NULL OR …`, every email reader tolerating NULL, and `password_hash` left NULL so the row\ncannot log in. Note `users_email_uniq` needs no change: Postgres treats NULLs as distinct, so many\nshadow rows per org are legal. A later genuine link must adopt the shadow rather than mint a second\nuser.","files":null}]}